## 0. Assumptions

I had no reporting-tool source code to look at, so this design rests on the assumptions below. If any is wrong, the listed sections need revisiting.

| # | Assumption | Sections affected if wrong |
|---|---|---|
| A1 | Server-side, multi-tenant web reporting service with a browser front end | 5, 6, 4.6 |
| A2 | Host and first-party SDK are TypeScript on Node.js; the wire protocol stays language-neutral | 7.6, 7.7, 9 |
| A3 | Plugin authors today are internal teams. Within about two years, semi-trusted third parties may be added without a redesign | 6.1, 10 |
| A4 | Deployment runs on Linux containers (e.g., Kubernetes) that can apply seccomp, cgroups, and network namespaces | 6.2 |

Names like `acme`, `rplug`, and `@reporting/*` are placeholders.

### Goals
- Five teams can build plugins **in parallel, against a frozen contract**, without coordinating with each other.
- A faulty plugin (crash, hang, leak, bad output) cannot take down the host, another plugin, or another tenant's data.
- Host releases don't break plugins, and plugin releases don't need host releases.
- Plugins can be upgraded and rolled back per tenant with zero downtime.

### Non-goals (v1)
- Plugin-to-plugin dependencies (see 4.9).
- A public or third-party marketplace, billing, or ratings.
- Plugins that change host UI chrome (navigation, auth screens). Plugins contribute only through the extension points in §2.

