# Reporting Tool Plugin Architecture: Design v1

## 0. Goals and non-goals

**Goals**
- Five teams can build plugins at the same time without coordinating with each other or waiting on the platform team.
- A bad plugin can't crash the host, read another tenant's data, get around user permissions, or use unbounded resources.
- Plugins built against API 1.x keep working on newer hosts during a published support window.
- Upgrades, rollbacks and reloads happen without restarting the host.

**Non-goals for v1**
- Plugins running inside the host process ("trusted mode"). This option is left out on purpose: once it exists, every team will ask for it.
- Plugins importing each other's code. Plugins talk to each other only through services the host brokers (§4.5).
- A public marketplace. The registry is internal, but its format allows publishing outside later.

---

## 1. Architecture overview

```
┌──────────────────────────── Host (reporting server) ────────────────────────────┐
│                                                                                  │
│  Report engine ──► Contribution Router ──► Plugin Supervisor ──► Plugin Host Pool │
│        ▲                 │                   (lifecycle,           │  per (plugin │
│        │                 ▼                    health, limits)      │  version,    │
│        │          Plugin Registry ◄── Registry Service (catalog,   │  tenant)     │
│        │          (installs, grants,      signed packages)         ▼              │
│        │           contribution index)                    ┌─────────────────┐     │
│        │                                                  │ Capability      │     │
│        └────────────── Capability Broker ◄── RPC (IPC) ───│ Broker endpoint │     │
│               (data, http, secrets, kv, logs, metrics)    └─────────────────┘     │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
          │ IPC socket only (no network, no host filesystem)
┌─────────▼──────────┐   ┌────────────────────┐        Browser
│ Plugin process A   │   │ Plugin process B   │   ┌─────────────────────────────┐
│ SDK runtime +      │   │                    │   │ Visualization plugins in    │
│ plugin bundle      │   │                    │   │ sandboxed iframes, bridged  │
└────────────────────┘   └────────────────────┘   │ to the app by postMessage   │
                                                  └─────────────────────────────┘
```

| Component | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| **Registry Service** | Stores signed, immutable plugin packages, version metadata, and yank/revoke status. |
| **Plugin Registry** (in the host) | Tracks installs per workspace, pinned versions, approved permissions, config, and an index of contributions. |
| **Plugin Supervisor** | Runs the lifecycle state machine, handles timeouts, crash restarts and quarantine, and runs blue/green reloads. |
| **Plugin Host Pool** | Runs one isolated OS process per plugin version per tenant. Evicts processes when idle. |
| **Capability Broker** | The only way a plugin reaches anything outside its process. Enforces permissions and quotas and writes the audit log. |
| **Contribution Router** | Maps a call such as "run data source `finance.gl/ledger`" to an active instance, and applies deadlines, cancellation and output validation. |

---

## 2. Extension points (contracts)

Each extension point has its own integer major version (`dataSource@1`). Section 7 explains why.

| Contract | Runs in | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `dataSource@1` | Server | Connects to an external system, describes its schema and streams query results. |
| `transform@1` | Server | Turns input record batches into output record batches (enrichment, pivots, forecasting). |
| `visualization@1` | Browser iframe | Renders a chart or widget from data plus options. |
| `exporter@1` | Server | Turns a rendered report into a byte stream (PDF, XLSX or a custom format). |
| `action@1` | Server | Reacts to events such as schedules, report completion or thresholds (delivery, alerts). |
| `service@1` | Server | Exposes a named RPC interface that other plugins can call through the host. |

**Tabular data crosses the boundary as Apache Arrow IPC record-batch streams**, never as JSON rows. Streams use backpressure and are referenced by stream handles, so they don't travel inline in RPC messages.

### 2.1 Contract interfaces (TypeScript, from `@corp/report-plugin-api`)

```ts
// ---------- shared ----------
interface CallContext {
  signal: AbortSignal;            // cancelled on deadline, user cancel, or unload
  deadline: number;               // epoch ms
  traceId: string;
  invoker: { userId: string; workspaceId: string; roles: readonly string[] };
  log: Logger;
}

type RecordBatchStream = AsyncIterable<import("apache-arrow").RecordBatch>;

// ---------- dataSource@1 ----------
interface DataSourceV1<Config = unknown> {
  testConnection(config: Config, call: CallContext): Promise<{ ok: true } | { ok: false; message: string }>;
  describe(config: Config, call: CallContext): Promise<SourceDescription>;   // tables, fields, pushdown capabilities
  query(req: QueryRequest<Config>, call: CallContext): RecordBatchStream;
}
interface SourceDescription {
  entities: Array<{ name: string; fields: FieldDef[] }>;
  pushdown: { filters: FilterOp[]; sort: boolean; limit: boolean; aggregate: boolean };
}
interface QueryRequest<Config> {
  config: Config; entity: string; fields: string[];
  filters?: Filter[]; sort?: SortSpec[]; limit?: number; params?: Record<string, Scalar>;
}
// The host re-applies any filter/sort/limit that the plugin did not declare in `pushdown`.

// ---------- transform@1 ----------
interface TransformV1<Options = unknown> {
  outputSchema(inputSchema: ArrowSchema, options: Options): Promise<ArrowSchema>;   // used for report design-time validation
  run(input: RecordBatchStream, options: Options, call: CallContext): RecordBatchStream;
}

// ---------- visualization@1 (browser) ----------
interface VisualizationV1<Options = unknown> {
  mount(el: HTMLElement, env: { theme: Theme; locale: string; bridge: VizBridge }): void | Promise<void>;
  render(data: ArrowTable, options: Options, size: { width: number; height: number }): void | Promise<void>;
  unmount(): void;
}
// VizBridge: emit interaction events (drilldown, selection) and request image export. It has no data access.

// ---------- exporter@1 ----------
interface ExporterV1<Options = unknown> {
  mimeType: string; fileExtension: string;
  export(report: RenderedReportHandle, options: Options, call: CallContext): AsyncIterable<Uint8Array>;
}

// ---------- action@1 ----------
interface ActionV1<Options = unknown> {
  run(event: ActionEvent, options: Options, call: CallContext): Promise<ActionResult>;
}

// ---------- service@1 ----------
interface ServiceV1 {
  methods: Record<string, (params: unknown, call: CallContext) => Promise<unknown>>;  // params/results validated by schemas in manifest
}
```

