## 0. Assumptions and scope

The working directory had no code, so nothing here is based on the existing codebase. The design rests on these assumptions. If any of them is wrong, the section named in brackets changes.

| # | Assumption | If wrong |
|---|---|---|
| A1 | Host backend is Node.js/TypeScript on Linux servers; developers may use macOS/Windows | §5.3 sandbox mechanics, §8 SDK |
| A2 | Multi-tenant SaaS: many customers (tenants) share one host | §5.5 tenant isolation |
| A3 | Plugin authors are internal teams. Their code is trusted not to be malicious, but not trusted to be bug-free or free of compromised dependencies | §5.1 threat model |
| A4 | Reports work on tabular data that can be large (millions of rows) | §6.4 data plane |
| A5 | Plugins run server-side. No plugin JavaScript runs in the end user's browser in v1 | §6.5 renderer contract |

**Goals**
1. Five teams can build, test, and ship plugins on their own, without changes to the host.
2. A faulty plugin cannot crash the host, block other plugins, or read another tenant's data.
3. A plugin written against API 1.x keeps working on every 1.x host, and on the 2.x host for at least 12 months.
4. Plugins can be installed, upgraded, rolled back, and removed at runtime with no host restart and no broken in-flight reports.

**Non-goals (v1)**
- Third-party/marketplace plugins from untrusted authors. §5.6 describes the upgrade path.
- Browser-side UI plugins that run arbitrary JS.
- Direct code sharing between plugins. Plugins share functionality only through host-mediated services (§6.6).

