## 0. Assumptions and open inputs

No host codebase was available when this was written, so these are assumptions. Check each one before freezing the design (§12).

| # | Assumption | If wrong |
|---|------------|----------|
| A1 | The host is a server-side service with a web UI. The first SDK is TypeScript on Node. | The contract (§7) doesn't depend on the language. Only the SDK and the sandbox runtime image (§6.2) change. |
| A2 | It can run multi-tenant (SaaS). A single-tenant deploy is the case where there is only one tenant. | Instance keys (§5.1) drop `tenantId`. Nothing else changes. |
| A3 | Plugins add data sources, transforms, visualizations, exporters, and delivery channels. | Extension points are a list you can add to (§2). Adding one is a minor API version. |
| A4 | The first plugins come from internal teams. Third-party plugins may come later. | Every plugin gets the same sandbox now, so opening to third parties later only changes signing and approval policy (§9). |
| A5 | Tabular data crosses the boundary in large volumes. | Arrow streaming (§7.4) could be swapped for JSON, but that costs performance. |

### Goals
- One team's plugin can't crash, slow down, or read data from the host, another plugin, or another tenant.
- Five teams can build in parallel against a stable, documented, testable contract.
- Plugins can be upgraded, rolled back, and reconfigured with no downtime. A failed reload never causes an outage.
- Saved reports keep working across plugin upgrades.

### Non-goals (v1)
- Direct plugin-to-plugin calls. Plugins compose through the host by contribution type (§4.5).
- A public marketplace or payments.
- Plugin runtimes other than Node. `wasm` and `python` are reserved in the manifest.

