## 1. Architecture overview

```
                           ┌──────────────────────── Host ────────────────────────┐
  CI / rplug publish ────▶ │  Catalog ──▶ Registry (per-tenant installs, grants)  │
                           │                  │                                   │
  Report engine / UI ────▶ │  Contribution Router ──▶ Supervisor ──▶ Instance Pool│
                           │        ▲                    │                        │
                           │        │             Capability Broker               │
                           │        │    (http, secrets, storage, data, log, ...) │
                           └────────┼────────────────────┼────────────────────────┘
                                    │  framed RPC over   │  per-instance
                                    │  UDS / named pipe  │  OS sandbox
                           ┌────────┴────────────────────┴───────┐
                           │  Plugin Instance (process)          │
                           │   plugin-runtime  ◀─▶  plugin code   │  x N
                           │   (SDK, protocol, handshake)        │
                           └─────────────────────────────────────┘

  Browser: report page ──postMessage──▶ sandboxed iframe (visualization UI bundle)
```

| Component | Responsibility |
|-----------|----------------|
| **Catalog** | Stores immutable published artifacts, keyed by sha256. Validates manifests, signatures, and API compatibility when an artifact is published. |
| **Registry** | Tracks installs per tenant: pinned version, admin-granted permissions, config, and enabled state. The single source of truth for "what should run." |
| **Contribution Router** | Maps a fully qualified contribution ID (`com.acme.sf/salesforce`) to a live instance. Triggers lazy activation. |
| **Supervisor** | Owns the lifecycle state machine (§5): spawning, health checks, draining, blue/green reload, crash backoff, quarantine. |
| **Capability Broker** | Host side of every host API call. Enforces grants, quotas, rate limits, and invoker identity. The plugin's only way to reach the outside world. |
| **plugin-runtime** | Host-owned code shipped in the sandbox image. Runs the handshake and protocol, then loads the plugin entry. Plugin authors never write it. |
| **SDK** (`@reporting/plugin-sdk`) | Typed wrapper that plugin authors use. It re-exports the types from `@reporting/plugin-api`. |

---

## 2. Extension points (contribution types)

A plugin contributes one or more **contributions**. Each contribution has a type, an ID that is unique within the plugin, and its own config schema and config version.

| Type | Purpose | Runs in | Idempotent by default |
|------|---------|---------|-----------------------|
| `dataSource` | Connect, discover schema, run queries that stream record batches | server sandbox | yes |
| `transform` | Map record batch streams to record batch streams (calculated columns, enrichment, reshaping) | server sandbox | yes |
| `visualization` | Optional server `prepare` step plus a client render bundle | server sandbox + browser iframe | yes |
| `exporter` | Turn a report snapshot into a file stream (PDF, XLSX, ...) | server sandbox | yes |
| `delivery` | Send an exported artifact to a destination (email, Slack, SFTP, ...) | server sandbox | **no** (the host supplies an idempotency key) |

The fully qualified contribution ID is `<pluginId>/<contributionId>`. Saved reports store the FQID, the plugin major version, and the contribution `configVersion`.

