## 0. Assumptions and scope

No host codebase was available, so this design assumes the following. Items marked **[confirm]** change parts of the design if wrong (see §12).

| Assumption | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| Host backend is Node/TypeScript on Linux in production **[confirm]** | Sandbox implementation (§6), SDK language |
| Web frontend renders reports and visualizations | UI sandbox (§6.6) |
| Multi-tenant SaaS: one host serves many customer tenants **[confirm]** | Tenant isolation, storage scoping |
| Plugin authors are internal teams, but plugins pull in third-party npm dependencies | Threat model (§6.1) |
| Tabular data is the main payload between host and plugins | Apache Arrow as the data format (§6.5) |

**In scope for v1:** registry, manifest, lifecycle (load/unload/reload), sandbox (server and UI), versioned API, SDK/CLI/test kit, publishing pipeline.

**Out of scope for v1 (decided, with reason):**
- **Plugin-to-plugin dependencies.** They multiply lifecycle complexity: reload ordering, dependency version conflicts, cascading failures. Put shared logic in npm libraries bundled into each plugin, or ask for it as a host capability. Revisit in v2.
- **In-process plugins and native addons.** They cannot be sandboxed.
- **External/third-party marketplace.** The trust model assumes a known publisher (§8.3).
- **Plugins changing host UI outside declared slots.**

