## 11. Key decisions and rejected alternatives

| Decision | Rejected alternative | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| Out-of-process sandbox with OS confinement | `worker_threads`, `vm`, isolated-vm in-process | Worker threads and `vm` are not security boundaries. In-process isolates share the host's crash domain and still need a separate I/O-mediation layer. |
| Node plugins in v1 | WASM components (Extism/wasmtime) | WASM is a stronger and lighter boundary, but the npm ecosystem (DB drivers, SDKs) and team skills make it costly now. The wire protocol is language-neutral, so a WASM or Python runtime can be added as another `runtime.kind`. |
| Capabilities bound to the invocation via `callId` | Plugin passes `tenantId` | Prevents confused-deputy and cross-tenant access, even from buggy plugins |
| Secrets injected by the host by default | Hand plaintext to plugins | Supply-chain code can't exfiltrate credentials it never sees |
| Arrow IPC with credit-based flow control | JSON rows | Memory and CPU at report-sized volumes; zero-copy into the UI |
| Blue/green reload | Stop-then-start | A failed upgrade never causes an outage |
| Contribution IDs namespaced by plugin | Global names with conflict resolution | Collisions are impossible by construction |
| Schema-first API with a CI compat checker | Hand-written TS types | Enforceable semver, and a future multi-language SDK |
| No plugin-to-plugin dependencies in v1 | Dependency graph with ordering | Large lifecycle/versioning complexity; five teams can share code through libraries |

