# Reporting Tool Plugin Architecture: v1 Design

## 1. Goals, non-goals, threat model

**Goals**
- Five teams can build plugins on their own against one stable contract.
- A broken plugin can't take down a report, another plugin, or the host.
- A plugin can only reach the data, network hosts and secrets it declared and an admin approved.
- Plugins can be loaded, unloaded and reloaded with zero downtime, and a failed reload rolls back.
- Reports saved today still open after plugins and the host are upgraded.

**Non-goals (on purpose)**
- **No calls between plugins or dependencies on other plugins.** Teams share code through ordinary npm libraries instead.
- **No in-process plugins.** Node `vm` and `worker_threads` are not security boundaries, and they share the host's crash domain.
- **No native addons and no `npm install` at runtime.** Dependencies are bundled at pack time.
- **No UI changes outside declared slots.**
- **No third-party marketplace.** See §13 Q5 for what changes if you add one.

**Threat model.** Plugin authors are internal: trusted but fallible. The design has to contain:
- **Faults:** crashes, memory leaks, hangs, runaway CPU.
- **Overreach:** a bug or a compromised npm dependency reaching undeclared data, hosts or secrets.
- **Data leakage:** sensitive report rows showing up in logs, error messages or other tenants.

It is *not* built to stop a determined hostile author with kernel exploits. §13 Q5 covers that upgrade.

## 2. Architecture overview

```
Browser                          Reporting Host (per replica)
┌──────────────────┐   ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Report UI        │   │ Report Engine ─► Contribution Index           │
│  ┌─────────────┐ │   │                        ▲                      │
│  │ sandboxed   │◄┼───┤ UI Bridge              │                      │
│  │ iframe (viz)│ │   │                  Runtime Registry ◄─ Package  │
│  └─────────────┘ │   │                  (install records)   Registry │
└──────────────────┘   │                        │                      │
                       │ Plugin Broker: routing, queues, timeouts,     │
                       │   quotas, permission checks, capability calls │
                       │                        │                      │
                       │ Supervisor: spawn / health / restart / kill   │
                       └───────┬───────────────────────┬───────────────┘
                     wire protocol (fd 3/4)           │
                       ┌───────▼───────┐       ┌───────▼───────┐
                       │ plugin process│       │ plugin process│  1 sandboxed
                       │ SDK + code    │       │ SDK + code    │  process per plugin
                       └───────┬───────┘       └───────────────┘
                               │ egress firewall + filtering DNS
                               ▼ (declared host:port only)
                         sap.internal:443
```

| Component | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| **Package Registry** | Stores signed plugin bundles. Handles publishing, release channels and permission review. Shared by all environments. |
| **Runtime Registry** | Runs in each host. Tracks install records, runs version resolution and the state machine, and builds the contribution index. |
| **Contribution Index** | Maps `kind + pluginId/contributionId` to a plugin, using manifests only, so the catalog works without starting any plugin. |
| **Broker** | Sits between the engine and plugins. Every call and every capability request goes through it and is checked there. |
| **Supervisor** | Owns the processes: sandbox setup, handshake, heartbeats, restarts, kills. |
| **SDK** (bundled in the plugin) | Speaks the wire protocol and exposes the typed API. Authors never see the protocol. |

## 3. Extension points

| Kind | What it does | Runs in |
|---|---|---|
| `dataSource` | Connects to an external system, discovers datasets and schema, streams query results | Plugin process |
| `transform` | Arrow batches in → Arrow batches out (pivot, forecast, enrichment) | Plugin process |
| `visualization` | Renders a tile. Optional server-side `prepare` step | Sandboxed iframe (+ plugin process) |
| `exporter` | Turns a rendered report into bytes (PDF, XLSX, …) | Plugin process |
| `action` | User- or schedule-triggered side effect (create ticket, push to Slack) | Plugin process |

Tabular data always crosses the boundary as **Apache Arrow IPC streams**: typed, columnar and streamable.

## 4. Manifest schema (`plugin.json`)

The TypeScript type, the JSON Schema (published at a stable URL for editor autocompletion) and the runtime validator are **all generated from one TypeBox/Zod definition** inside `@reporting/plugin-api`. They can't drift apart.

```ts
interface PluginManifestV1 {
  $schema?: string;
  manifestVersion: 1;
  id: string;            // reverse-DNS, /^[a-z][a-z0-9]*(\.[a-z][a-z0-9-]*){2,5}$/, ≤128, immutable forever
  name: string;          // 1–64 chars
  version: string;       // strict semver
  description?: string;  // ≤512
  icon?: BundlePath;
  publisher: { team: string /* /^[a-z][a-z0-9-]{1,63}$/ */; contact: string /* email */; repository?: string };

  engines: { pluginApi: string };        // "^1.2.0" | "~1.2.0" | "1.2.0" — must stay within one major
  runtime: { type: 'node'; version?: string /* major, e.g. "22" */; entry: BundlePath };
  ui?: { entry: BundlePath };            // required iff visualizations exist

  activation?: ActivationEvent[];        // default ['onUse']
  // 'onStartup' | 'onUse' | `onSchedule:${id}` |
  // `on${'DataSource'|'Transform'|'Visualization'|'Exporter'|'Action'}:${contributionId}`

  contributes: {                          // at least one
    dataSources?:    (ContributionBase & { supportsPushdown?: ('filter'|'projection'|'sort'|'limit'|'aggregate')[];
                                           supportsIncremental?: boolean })[];
    transforms?:     (ContributionBase & { arity?: 'unary' | 'nary' })[];
    visualizations?: (ContributionBase & { uiComponent: string; hasBackend?: boolean;
                                           dataShape?: { minColumns?: number; maxColumns?: number; maxRows?: number /* ≤1e6, default 50k */ } })[];
    exporters?:      (ContributionBase & { mimeType: string; fileExtension: string; needsRenderedVisuals?: boolean })[];
    actions?:        (ContributionBase & { placement: ('reportToolbar'|'tileMenu'|'rowContext'|'schedule')[];
                                           confirmation?: string })[];
  };

  permissions?: {                         // anything not listed is denied
    network?: { hosts: string[] };        // "host:port"; one leading "*." allowed; bare "*" and IP literals rejected
    secrets?: string[];                   // logical names, bound to vault paths at install
    storage?: { kvQuotaMb?: number /* ≤1024 */; scratchQuotaMb?: number /* ≤4096 */ };
    reportData?: 'none' | 'read';         // default 'none' (schemas only, never rows)
    userIdentity?: 'none' | 'basic' | 'email';  // default 'basic' (id, locale, tz). Never tokens.
  };

  resources?: {
    memoryMb?: number;           // 64–4096, default 256
    cpu?: number;                // cores, 0.1–4, default 0.5
    maxConcurrentCalls?: number; // default 16
    callTimeoutMs?: number;      // default 30_000, max 600_000
    loadTimeoutMs?: number;      // default 10_000
    unloadTimeoutMs?: number;    // default 5_000
    idleUnloadSec?: number;      // default 900, 0 = never
  };

  configSchema?: BundlePath;     // install-time config (JSON Schema 2020-12)
  experimental?: boolean;        // unlocks ctx.experimental; rejected on the stable channel
}

interface ContributionBase {
  id: string;                    // /^[a-z][a-zA-Z0-9-]{1,63}$/, unique per kind within the plugin
  displayName: string;
  description?: string;
  optionsSchema?: BundlePath;    // per-use options stored inside saved reports
  optionsVersion?: number;       // int ≥1; bump on incompatible optionsSchema change → must implement migrateOptions
}

type BundlePath = string;        // relative POSIX path inside the bundle; no "..", no leading "/"
```

**Example**

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://reporting.internal/schemas/plugin-manifest/v1.json",
  "manifestVersion": 1,
  "id": "com.acme.finance.sap-connector",
  "name": "SAP HANA Connector",
  "version": "2.3.1",
  "publisher": { "team": "finance-data", "contact": "finance-data@acme.com" },
  "engines": { "pluginApi": "^1.2.0" },
  "runtime": { "type": "node", "version": "22", "entry": "dist/index.js" },
  "activation": ["onDataSource:sap-hana"],
  "contributes": {
    "dataSources": [{
      "id": "sap-hana",
      "displayName": "SAP HANA",
      "optionsSchema": "schemas/sap-hana-options.json",
      "optionsVersion": 2,
      "supportsPushdown": ["projection", "filter", "limit"]
    }]
  },
  "permissions": {
    "network": { "hosts": ["hana.finance.acme.internal:30015"] },
    "secrets": ["hana-credentials"],
    "reportData": "read"
  },
  "resources": { "memoryMb": 512, "callTimeoutMs": 120000 }
}
```

**Semantic checks beyond the schema** (run by `rplugin validate`, again at publish, and again at install):
1. Every path exists in the bundle. Option and config schemas are valid 2020-12 and contain no remote `$ref`.
2. `ui` is present if and only if there are visualizations. Each `uiComponent` is actually exported by `ui.entry`.
3. `engines.pluginApi` resolves to exactly one major that the target host supports.
4. A published `id@version` is immutable. `id` can't be reused by another team.
5. **Permission diff** against the previous published version: any widening is flagged for security review (§5).
6. `experimental: true` is rejected on the `stable` channel.
7. At runtime, anything registered in `onLoad` that isn't declared in the manifest is rejected, and anything declared but not registered fails the load. **The manifest is the source of truth.**

## 5. Plugin registry

### 5.1 Bundle format (`.rplugin`)
A tar.gz containing:
- `plugin.json`, `dist/` (all dependencies bundled), `ui/`, `schemas/`
- `sbom.cdx.json` (CycloneDX)
- `CONTENTS` (a SHA-256 of every file)
- `SIGNATURE` (the registry's signature over the digest of `CONTENTS`)

Install scripts never run on hosts.

### 5.2 Publish pipeline (Package Registry)
`rplugin publish`, then CI gates:
- Schema and semantic validation
- The conformance kit (§10) against every supported host API minor
- Dependency vulnerability audit
- Bundle size limit (default 50 MB)
- Permission diff

**Widened permissions?** The version is held until a security reviewer approves. **Otherwise** it's signed and released to a channel:

| Channel | Accepted by |
|---|---|
| `dev` | Dev hosts. Unsigned local bundles are also allowed in `rplugin dev`. |
| `staging` | Staging hosts |
| `stable` | Production hosts |

Promotion only ever goes forward and never rebuilds, so the same digest moves through every channel.

### 5.3 Install records (Runtime Registry storage)

```sql
plugin_installations (
  tenant_id            text,          -- or constant if single-tenant (§13 Q3)
  plugin_id            text,
  version              text,
  digest               text,          -- pinned; verified on every process start
  channel              text,
  enabled              boolean,
  config               jsonb,         -- validated against configSchema
  secret_bindings      jsonb,         -- { "hana-credentials": "vault://finance/hana/prod" }
  permissions_granted  jsonb,         -- snapshot approved by admin
  state                text,          -- see §6
  last_error           jsonb,
  previous_version     text,          -- rollback target
  installed_by text, installed_at timestamptz, updated_at timestamptz,
  PRIMARY KEY (tenant_id, plugin_id)
)
```

**Effective permissions = declared ∩ granted.** If an upgrade declares new permissions, the install stays on the old version until an admin grants them.

- **Auto-update policy** (per install): patch versions may auto-update within a channel. Minor and major versions need manual approval. So does any permission widening, always.

### 5.4 Resolution (on host start and on every install change)
1. Load the install records. Download any missing bundle into the local cache and verify its signature and digest (mismatch → `FAILED: INTEGRITY`).
2. Validate the manifest and check it's compatible with the host's supported API majors (mismatch → `FAILED: INCOMPATIBLE_API` at install time, never discovered mid-report).
3. Check that config validates and every declared secret is bound (otherwise `FAILED: MISCONFIGURED`).
4. Add the contributions to the index under fully qualified refs (`com.acme.finance.sap-connector/sap-hana`). Refs never collide, because plugin IDs are unique.
5. Mark the plugin `RESOLVED`. No process starts until an activation event fires.

### 5.5 Saved reports and plugin references
Saved reports store references, never implementation details:

```json
{ "kind": "dataSource", "ref": "com.acme.finance.sap-connector/sap-hana",
  "optionsVersion": 1, "options": { "schema": "FIN", "table": "GL" } }
```

- **Stored `optionsVersion` is older than the manifest's:** the host calls the provider's `migrateOptions` when the report opens, uses the result, and persists it on the next save. Migrations must handle **every** earlier version. The conformance kit replays stored fixtures to check this.
- **Plugin missing, disabled or failed:** the report still opens. Only the affected tiles show a "Plugin unavailable" placeholder, and their options are kept untouched.

### 5.6 Admin API

```
GET    /api/plugins                         catalog + state + health
GET    /api/plugins/{id}                    manifest, install record, metrics summary
POST   /api/plugins/{id}/install            { version, channel, config, secretBindings, grant }
POST   /api/plugins/{id}/upgrade            { version, grant? }         → reload path (§6.4)
POST   /api/plugins/{id}/rollback                                        → previous_version
POST   /api/plugins/{id}/enable | disable
POST   /api/plugins/{id}/reload                                          → same version, fresh process
PUT    /api/plugins/{id}/config             → onConfigChange or reload
DELETE /api/plugins/{id}                    uninstall (kv data retained 30 days)
POST   /api/plugins/{id}/unquarantine
```

Every change writes an audit event. With multiple host replicas, a change updates the record and publishes an event, and each replica runs its own local transition. For a short window different replicas can run different versions, which is one reason §8.3 requires compatibility within a major.

## 6. Lifecycle

### 6.1 State machine

```
INSTALLED ─validate─► RESOLVED ─activation event─► STARTING ─handshake─► LOADING ─onLoad ok─► ACTIVE
                          ▲                            │                    │                 │  ▲
                          │                            └──────fail──────────┴──► FAILED       │  │ reload ok / rollback
                          │                                                     │  ▲         ▼  │
                          │                                         backoff ────┘  │      RELOADING
                          └──────── UNLOADING ◄── disable / idle / uninstall ── ACTIVE
FAILED ×5 in 10 min (or 3 consecutive load failures) ─► QUARANTINED ─admin─► RESOLVED
DISABLED: admin-set, reachable from any state
```

### 6.2 Hooks

| Hook | Required | When | Timeout | If it fails |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `onLoad(ctx)` | yes | After handshake, before any calls. Register every contribution here. `ctx.handoff` is set on reload. | `loadTimeoutMs` | Process killed → `FAILED` |
| `onUnload(ctx)` | no | Before process exit. `ctx.reason`: `disable`, `uninstall`, `upgrade`, `reload`, `idle` or `shutdown` | `unloadTimeoutMs` | Ignored: SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after 2 s |
| `onSnapshot()` | no | During reload, on the **old** instance. Returns handoff state (JSON, ≤1 MiB) | 2 s | Handoff omitted, reload continues |
| `onConfigChange(next, prev)` | no | Admin changes config. Return `'applied'`, or `'reload'` to request a full reload | 5 s | Treated as `'reload'` |

**Rules**
- **Cleanup doesn't depend on the plugin cooperating.** Everything dies with the process, and the host revokes the plugin's kv sessions and secret leases.
- `onLoad` must not take exclusive external locks, because two instances overlap during reload (§6.4).
- Handoff state is a hint. The new instance must work when `ctx.handoff` is missing, or when it comes from an older version (`ctx.handoff.fromVersion`).

### 6.3 Load and unload sequences

**Load**
1. The Supervisor builds the sandbox and spawns the process.
2. `hello`/`welcome` handshake (§9).
3. The host sends `load` with config and optional handoff, and the SDK runs `onLoad`.
4. The SDK reports what was registered, and the host checks it against the manifest.
5. `ACTIVE`: the broker starts routing.

**Unload**
1. Broker stops routing new calls; they get `PLUGIN_UNAVAILABLE`, or queue if this unload is part of a reload.
2. In-flight calls drain until the shorter of `callTimeoutMs` and 30 s.
3. Calls still running get an abort on `call.signal`, then `CANCELLED`.
4. `onUnload`, then `onDispose` callbacks, then SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after 2 s.
5. The scratch directory is wiped.

### 6.4 Reload (upgrade, config reload, dev hot reload): blue/green with rollback

1. **Prepare.** Verify and resolve the new bundle. If this fails, the old instance is untouched.
2. **Spawn the new process** and handshake. `onLoad` doesn't run yet.
3. **Pause the old instance.**
   - New calls queue in the broker (up to 1000 calls, each waiting at most `loadTimeoutMs`).
   - In-flight calls drain as in §6.3. Anything cancelled here gets the retryable error `PLUGIN_RELOADING`.
4. **Snapshot:** `old.onSnapshot()` produces the handoff state.
5. **Load:** `new.onLoad(ctx)` runs with `ctx.handoff = { fromVersion, state }`.
6. **Result:**
   - **Success:** queued calls go to the new instance, `old.onUnload({ reason: 'reload' })` runs, and the old process is killed. The install record is committed.
   - **Failure:** the new process is killed and the old instance **resumes**. It was only paused, never unloaded. The install record reverts to `previous_version`, and an alert fires.

`rplugin dev` hot reload uses this exact path, so teams exercise it every day.

### 6.5 Crashes and hangs
- **Detection:**
  - Process exit or OOM kill
  - **Hang:** 3 missed heartbeats (a heartbeat is sent every 5 s)
  - **Leaked calls:** more than 10 calls that ignored cancellation for over 2 s. The process is recycled.
- **In-flight calls:**
  - Queries, transforms and exports fail with `PLUGIN_CRASHED` (retryable). The engine retries each one once.
  - Actions fail with `PLUGIN_CRASHED` (not retryable) and are never retried automatically. `idempotencyKey` lets a manual retry be safe.
- **Restart backoff:** 1 s, 2 s, 4 s, … up to 60 s. Crash-looping sends the plugin to `QUARANTINED` (§6.1).
- **Scope:** a crash affects only that plugin's tiles, never the whole report.

## 7. Sandboxed execution boundary

### 7.1 Why processes, not WASM or in-process
Teams will need existing npm database drivers, Arrow and PDF libraries. A WASM component model would isolate better and start faster, but today it rules out most of those drivers. The wire protocol doesn't care what language is on the other side, so `runtime.type: "wasm"` or `"python"` can be added later without an API break.

### 7.2 Layers (each one assumes the one above can fail)

| # | Layer | Enforces |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Process per plugin** (per plugin and tenant if multi-tenant) | Separate crash and memory domain |
| 2 | **OS container** | Unprivileged per-plugin UID. Read-only root filesystem, bundle mounted read-only. Writable tmpfs scratch with quota. cgroups v2 limits on `memory.max`, `cpu.max` and `pids.max` (64). No service-account token mounted. Environment scrubbed except `TZ`, `LANG` and SDK variables. seccomp default profile. No capabilities (`cap_drop: ALL`). |
| 3 | **Network** | Separate network namespace. Egress firewall plus a filtering DNS resolver that only resolves and allows `permissions.network.hosts`. On Kubernetes, FQDN network policies (e.g. Cilium). Always blocked: link-local and metadata (169.254.0.0/16), host internal APIs, other plugins. **Enforced at the network layer, so ordinary TCP database drivers work unmodified.** |
| 4 | **Node permission model** | `--permission --allow-fs-read=<bundle>,<scratch> --allow-fs-write=<scratch>`; no child processes, workers, addons or WASI. Node's docs describe this as a "seat belt" and **not** a boundary against malicious code, so it's defense in depth only. |
| 5 | **Host-side capability checks** | Every capability RPC (secrets, kv, identity, report data) is checked in the broker against effective permissions. Plugin identity comes from **which pipe** the message arrived on, never from a field in the message. |
| 6 | **Data minimization** | Plugins receive only the data passed into a call, with row-level security already applied for the calling user. `reportData: 'none'` plugins get schemas, never rows. No plugin can query arbitrary datasets. |

### 7.3 Secrets
- **Access:** only through `ctx.secrets.get(name)`, and only for declared names. The value is resolved from the vault per install.
- **Caching:** cached in SDK memory for up to 5 minutes.
- **Never:** in environment variables, the manifest, handoff state or kv.
- **Logs:** the host redacts known secret values from the plugin's log stream, a backstop rather than the main control.

### 7.4 Resource limits

| Limit | Default | Enforced by |
|---|---|---|
| Memory | 256 MiB | cgroup + `--max-old-space-size` at 80% |
| CPU | 0.5 core | cgroup |
| Concurrent calls | 16 | Broker (excess queues, 429-style `QUOTA_EXCEEDED` after 5 s) |
| Call timeout | 30 s | Broker → abort, then `CALL_TIMEOUT` |
| Frame size | 8 MiB | Wire protocol |
| Result size | 2 GiB per query, 1M rows per visualization | Broker |
| kv quota | 10 MiB | Host |
| Scratch disk | 256 MiB | tmpfs size |
| Log rate | 100 lines/s, then sampled | Supervisor |

### 7.5 UI sandbox (visualizations)
- **Iframe:** `<iframe sandbox="allow-scripts">`. No `allow-same-origin`, forms, popups or top navigation.
- **Separate origin:** `https://plugin-ui.reporting.internal/<pluginId>/<digest>/`.
- **CSP:** `default-src 'none'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'none'`. A visualization has **no network access**, so all data arrives through the bridge.
- **Bridge:**
  - A `MessageChannel` port is transferred at init, and the host validates every message against a schema.
  - The host provides input data (capped by `dataShape.maxRows`), options, theme, locale and size.
  - The plugin may send: `select`/`drilldown` events, `renderComplete()` and `reportError()`.
- **Headless export:** the same iframe loads in headless Chromium, and the exporter waits for `renderComplete` (10 s timeout, then a placeholder image).

### 7.6 Dev mode
`rplugin dev` on Windows or macOS runs layers 1, 4, 5, 6 and the UI sandbox, but not layers 2–3. It shows a `SANDBOX: REDUCED` banner. CI conformance runs the full Linux sandbox, so a network or filesystem violation is caught before staging, not in production.

## 8. Versioned API contract

### 8.1 Packages
| Package | Contents |
|---|---|
| `@reporting/plugin-api` | Types, manifest schema and validator. **This is the contract.** |
| `@reporting/plugin-sdk` | Server runtime: `definePlugin`, wire protocol, heartbeats |
| `@reporting/plugin-ui-sdk` | `defineVisualization`, iframe bridge |
| `@reporting/plugin-testing` | Mock `ctx`/`CallContext`, Arrow builders, fake clock |
| `@reporting/plugin-conformance` | Contract test kit (§10) |

### 8.2 The API (v1.0.0)

```ts
// ───── Entry ─────
export function definePlugin(def: PluginDefinition): PluginDefinition;

export interface PluginDefinition {
  onLoad(ctx: PluginContext): void | Promise<void>;
  onUnload?(ctx: UnloadContext): void | Promise<void>;
  onSnapshot?(): Json | Promise<Json>;
  onConfigChange?(next: Json, prev: Json): ConfigChangeResult | Promise<ConfigChangeResult>;
}
export type ConfigChangeResult = 'applied' | 'reload';
export interface UnloadContext {
  readonly reason: 'disable' | 'uninstall' | 'upgrade' | 'reload' | 'idle' | 'shutdown' | (string & {});
  readonly deadline: number;
}

// ───── Plugin context (lives for the life of the instance) ─────
export interface PluginContext {
  readonly plugin: { readonly id: string; readonly version: string };
  readonly api: { readonly version: string; has(feature: ApiFeature): boolean };
  readonly config: Json;                               // already validated against configSchema
  readonly handoff?: { readonly fromVersion: string; readonly state: Json };
  readonly log: Logger;
  readonly metrics: Metrics;
  readonly secrets: Secrets;
  readonly kv: KeyValueStore;
  readonly scratchDir: string;
  readonly register: Registrar;
  onDispose(fn: () => void | Promise<void>): void;
  readonly experimental?: unknown;                     // present only if manifest.experimental
}

export interface Registrar {
  dataSource<O = Json>(id: string, impl: DataSourceProvider<O>): void;
  transform<O = Json>(id: string, impl: TransformProvider<O>): void;
  exporter<O = Json>(id: string, impl: ExporterProvider<O>): void;
  action<O = Json>(id: string, impl: ActionProvider<O>): void;
  visualizationBackend<O = Json>(id: string, impl: VisualizationBackend<O>): void;
}

// ───── Per-call context ─────
export interface CallContext {
  readonly requestId: string;
  readonly traceparent: string;        // W3C
  readonly tenantId: string;
  readonly signal: AbortSignal;        // cancel, timeout, unload — MUST be honored within 2 s
  readonly deadline: number;           // epoch ms
  readonly user?: UserInfo;            // shape depends on permissions.userIdentity
  readonly log: Logger;                // pre-tagged with requestId
}
export interface UserInfo { readonly id: string; readonly locale: string; readonly timeZone: string; readonly email?: string }

// ───── Options lifecycle (shared by all contribution kinds) ─────
export interface OptionsAware<O> {
  /** Required if manifest optionsVersion > 1. Must accept every earlier version. */
  migrateOptions?(fromVersion: number, options: Json, call: CallContext): O | Promise<O>;
  /** Checks beyond JSON Schema (e.g. table exists). */
  validateOptions?(options: O, call: CallContext): ValidationIssue[] | Promise<ValidationIssue[]>;
}
export interface ValidationIssue { readonly path: string; readonly message: string; readonly severity: 'error' | 'warning' }

// ───── Data source ─────
export interface DataSourceProvider<O = Json> extends OptionsAware<O> {
  testConnection(options: O, call: CallContext): Promise<{ ok: true } | { ok: false; message: string }>;
  discover(options: O, call: CallContext): Promise<DatasetInfo[]>;
  getSchema(options: O, dataset: string, call: CallContext): Promise<ArrowSchema>;
  query(req: QueryRequest<O>, call: CallContext): Promise<QueryResult>;
}
export interface DatasetInfo { readonly name: string; readonly displayName?: string; readonly description?: string }
export interface QueryRequest<O> {
  readonly options: O;
  readonly dataset: string;
  readonly params: Readonly<Record<string, Scalar>>;
  readonly pushdown: {                 // only kinds declared in manifest.supportsPushdown are ever sent
    readonly projection?: readonly string[];
    readonly filter?: FilterExpr;
    readonly sort?: readonly SortKey[];
    readonly limit?: number;
    readonly aggregate?: AggregateSpec;
  };
  readonly incremental?: { readonly cursor: string };  // only if supportsIncremental
}
export interface QueryResult {
  readonly schema: ArrowSchema;
  readonly applied: readonly PushdownKind[];           // engine applies whatever was not applied
  readonly batches: AsyncIterable<RecordBatch>;
  readonly nextCursor?: () => string | undefined;      // read after batches are exhausted
}

// ───── Transform ─────
export interface TransformProvider<O = Json> extends OptionsAware<O> {
  outputSchema(inputs: readonly ArrowSchema[], options: O, call: CallContext): Promise<ArrowSchema>;
  transform(inputs: readonly AsyncIterable<RecordBatch>[], options: O, call: CallContext): AsyncIterable<RecordBatch>;
}

// ───── Visualization (server half, optional) ─────
export interface VisualizationBackend<O = Json> extends OptionsAware<O> {
