## 4. Plugin registry

### 4.1 Responsibilities
- Store packages and their metadata, and verify integrity and signatures.
- Validate manifests (§3) and compute the permission diff between versions.
- Track installed versions, the active version per environment, enablement per workspace, and approved grants.
- Resolve service dependencies and activation order.
- Provide the contribution index the rest of the host queries, e.g. "which exporters support `application/pdf` in workspace W?". The index is built from manifests only, so no plugin code runs.

### 4.2 Package format (`.rpx` = zip)

```
plugin.json          manifest (§3)
dist/                bundled code (single-file bundle recommended; no node_modules install at host)
assets/              icons, web bundles
sbom.cdx.json        CycloneDX SBOM
CHANGELOG.md
SIGNATURE            detached signature over the sha256 of the canonical archive listing
```

- Packages are built and signed only in CI, with a per-team key (Sigstore/cosign or an internal KMS). The registry pins which signer identities may publish under which `id` prefix. For example, `com.acme.finance.*` can only be published by the `finance-data` signer.
- Native addons (`.node`) are rejected in v1, because they bypass the runtime permission layer.
- Install-time scripts don't exist: the host never runs `npm install`.

### 4.3 Sources

| Source | Environments | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Bundled | all | Ships with the host, e.g. core CSV/PDF exporters. These are signed by the platform team. |
| Org registry (artifact repo) | all | This is the normal route. |
| Local directory | dev only | Unsigned; hot reload on file change; the host shows a "DEV" banner and refuses to start with it in prod. |

### 4.4 Install and upgrade pipeline

```
upload/publish
  → verify digest + signature + signer↔id-prefix binding        (fail → REJECTED)
  → schema + semantic validation (§3.2, §3.3)                    (fail → INVALID)
  → hostApi compatibility check                                  (fail → INCOMPATIBLE)
  → SBOM vulnerability policy (no critical CVEs, configurable)   (fail → QUARANTINED)
  → conformance report present & passing for this version (§10)  (fail → QUARANTINED)
  → permission diff vs currently approved grant
        no new permissions  → AVAILABLE (auto-approvable per env policy)
        new permissions     → PENDING_APPROVAL (platform/security approver)
  → admin activates version in environment → lifecycle (§5)
```

Versions can be **yanked**: a yanked version can't be newly activated, but a running one keeps going until an admin moves off it. Rollback means activating a previous available version, which uses the reload path in §5.4.

### 4.5 Data model

```ts
interface PluginRecord {
  id: string;
  ownerTeam: string;
  versions: PluginVersionRecord[];
  environments: Record<EnvName, {
    activeVersion: string | null;
    enablement: { mode: "all-workspaces" } | { mode: "allowlist"; workspaceIds: string[] };
    grant: PermissionGrant;             // approved permissions for activeVersion
    installConfig: ConfigRef;           // secrets stored as vault refs
    adminState: "enabled" | "disabled";
  }>;
}

interface PluginVersionRecord {
  version: string;
  digest: string;                       // sha256
  signer: string;
  publishedAt: string;
  manifest: Manifest;
  status: "AVAILABLE" | "PENDING_APPROVAL" | "INVALID" | "INCOMPATIBLE" | "QUARANTINED" | "REJECTED" | "YANKED";
  conformance: { hostApi: string; passed: boolean; reportUrl: string };
}

interface PermissionGrant {
  network: { allow: string[]; allowPrivate: boolean };
  secrets: string[];
  storage: { kvQuotaMb: number; blobQuotaMb: number };
  data: DataScope[];
  tmpDiskMb: number;
  approvedBy: string; approvedAt: string;
}
```

Workspace-level config, including the `connection` objects of data sources, lives with the workspace's own data. It's stored as `{ pluginId, contributionId, specVersion, value }` so it can be migrated (§7.8).

### 4.6 Dependency resolution
- Plugins depend on **service interfaces** (`requires.services`), never on other plugin IDs. Any active plugin that provides a matching `interface@version` satisfies the dependency, so teams stay decoupled.
- The resolver builds a graph over active plugins. A missing non-optional provider puts the dependent in `UNRESOLVED`. A cycle puts every member of the cycle in `UNRESOLVED`. Neither affects unrelated plugins.
- If more than one provider matches, the highest `interfaceVersion` wins, and ties are broken by an admin-set preference. The choice is recorded and shown.
- Providers activate before their dependents. Deactivating a provider first drains its dependents' in-flight calls to it, and then marks those dependents `UNRESOLVED` unless the dependency is optional.

### 4.7 Registry admin API (HTTP + `rpx` CLI)

```
POST   /plugins/packages                         publish (.rpx)
GET    /plugins                                  list + states
GET    /plugins/{id}/versions/{v}                manifest, status, permission diff
POST   /plugins/{id}/versions/{v}:approve        approve grant  (role: plugin-approver)
POST   /envs/{env}/plugins/{id}:activate         { version }     → load or reload
POST   /envs/{env}/plugins/{id}:reload           same version, new generation
POST   /envs/{env}/plugins/{id}:disable | :enable
PUT    /envs/{env}/plugins/{id}/config           install-level config
POST   /plugins/{id}/versions/{v}:yank
GET    /envs/{env}/contributions?point=exporters&workspace=W
GET    /envs/{env}/plugins/{id}/runtime          generations, instances, health, restarts
```

