## 5. Sandboxed execution boundary

### 5.1 Why out of process

| Option | Security boundary | Crash isolation | Language-neutral | Cost |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| In-process module | None | No | No | Lowest |
| Language VM isolate (e.g. `isolated-vm`) | Partial | Partial | No | Low |
| WASM/WASI | Strong | Yes | Partly (DB drivers and native libraries are hard) | Low–medium |
| **OS process + OS sandbox** | **Strong** | **Yes** | **Yes** | IPC + serialization |

Datasource plugins need real database drivers. Five teams are unlikely to share a language. That
points to OS processes. The serialization cost is contained by streaming Arrow batches (§8.5).
`runtime.kind: "wasm"` is reserved for `function` and `transform` plugins in a later minor, and
adding it will not change the API.

### 5.2 Sandbox requirements (normative: any implementation must meet all of these)

| Area | Requirement | Reference implementation (Linux) |
|---|---|---|
| Filesystem | Package is mounted read-only. The only writable path is scratch space (`scratchDiskMb`, tmpfs or quota-limited volume, wiped on stop). No host paths are visible. | Container with read-only rootfs, bind-mounted package, tmpfs `/scratch` |
| Processes | Runs as a non-root UID with no capabilities and `no_new_privs`. Child processes are allowed only inside the same cgroup. | seccomp default profile; gVisor (`runsc`) recommended |
| Memory / CPU | Hard memory limit at `memoryMb`, where an OOM kill moves the instance to FAILED. CPU quota at `cpu`. PID limit of 256. | cgroup v2 |
| Network | **Denied by default.** Egress is allowed only to `capabilities.network.egress` hosts and ports, through a per-instance egress proxy. DNS goes through a host resolver that answers only for allowlisted names. Private, loopback, and link-local ranges are blocked unless `allowPrivate` is approved. `169.254.169.254` and other cloud metadata endpoints are **always** blocked. | Separate network namespace; only route is to an egress proxy that enforces SNI/host+port per instance |
| Environment | Environment variables are empty apart from `REPORTING_PLUGIN_FD`, `TZ=UTC`, and `LANG=C.UTF-8`. Secrets are **never** passed in env or on disk. | — |
| Host channel | One Unix domain socket (named pipe on Windows) per instance, passed as an inherited file descriptor. The handshake must present a one-time instance token. | — |
| Time | The heartbeat is `$/ping` every 10 s. Three misses move the instance to FAILED. | — |

### 5.3 Isolation key and multi-tenancy

- `isolation: "per-tenant"` is the default. Each tenant gets its own instance, so no tenant data ever shares a process's memory with another tenant's.
- `isolation: "shared"` means one instance serves all tenants. It requires explicit admin approval and is meant only for plugins that never touch tenant data or tenant secrets, such as a pure formatting function.
- In a single-tenant deployment the isolation key is always `shared` and the setting has no effect.

### 5.4 Capability broker (host side)

Every host API a plugin calls is an RPC that the broker handles. The broker **re-checks every call**
against the approved capabilities and never trusts the plugin.

| Host API | Capability check | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `ctx.http.fetch` | The URL's host and port must match the egress allowlist. | This is the preferred route for HTTP: it is audited, redirects are re-checked, and the response size limit is 64 MiB. |
| Raw TCP (drivers) | Enforced by the egress proxy. | Only for drivers that cannot use `ctx.http`. |
| `ctx.secrets.get(name)` | `name` must be in `capabilities.secrets`. | Resolved from the host vault on each call. The value is **added to the log redaction set** for that instance. |
| `ctx.storage.*` | `storage` must be declared. | Scoped to `(pluginId, isolationKey)`. Quota enforced. Keys ≤ 256 B, values ≤ 1 MiB. |
| `call.datasets.read(ref)` | `datasets.read` must be declared **and** the call grant must cover `ref`. | The invoker's row-level and column-level security is applied **before** any data crosses into the sandbox. |
| `call.reports.metadata(ref)` | `reports.metadata.read` plus the grant. | — |
| `call.notifications.send` | `notifications.send` plus the grant. | Rate-limited per tenant. |
| `ctx.log` / `ctx.metrics` | Always allowed. | Rate-limited to 1000 log lines/min per instance; the excess is dropped and counted. Secrets are redacted. |

**Grants prevent confused-deputy attacks.** APIs that act on user data exist only on `CallContext`,
not on the plugin-wide `ctx`. A grant = invoker permissions ∩ plugin capabilities. It expires when
the call ends or its deadline passes. Plugins cannot store and reuse a grant.

### 5.5 Per-call limits

| Limit | Default | Manifest override max |
|---|---|---|
| `testConnection` | 30 s | 120 s |
| `discoverSchema` | 60 s | 300 s |
| `query` / `execute`: time to first batch | 60 s | 600 s |
| `query` / `execute`: idle time between batches | 60 s | 600 s |
| `render` | 10 s | 30 s |
| `export` | 5 min | 30 min |
| `deliver` | 2 min | 10 min |
| `function.evaluate` (per batch) | 5 s | 30 s |
| `migrateConfig` | 5 s | 5 s |
| Concurrent calls per instance | `maxConcurrentCalls` (8) | 64 |
| JSON message size | 4 MiB | — |
| Binary frame size | 16 MiB | — |

Total run time is capped by the host's report-run limit, not by the plugin.

**Cancellation.** The host sends `$/cancel`, which aborts `call.signal`. The plugin must stop within
**5 s**. After that, the host abandons the call and the caller gets `CANCELLED`. If more than 4
abandoned calls are still running in one instance, the host recycles it: new instance, drain the old
one.

### 5.6 Frontend boundary (iframe visualizations)

`renderer: "spec"` is the default and the preferred option. The plugin returns a declarative spec
(§7.5), and the host renders it with its own trusted renderer, so plugin code never runs in the
browser.

`renderer: "iframe"` is for visuals a spec cannot express:

- `<iframe sandbox="allow-scripts">`. It does **not** get `allow-same-origin`, so it has an opaque origin with no cookies and no storage.
- It is served from a separate origin that exists only for plugin UI.
- CSP: `default-src 'none'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; connect-src 'none'`.
- The iframe gets data only through a versioned `postMessage` bridge (`ui.render`, `ui.resize`, `ui.event`), and the host validates every message.
- The UI cannot reach the network or host APIs. Any data it needs is fetched by the server-side plugin.

### 5.7 Threat model

| Threat | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Plugin reads another tenant's data | Per-tenant instances; data only through call grants; row-level security applied host-side before data crosses |
| Data exfiltration | Network denied by default; egress allowlist approved by admin; broker audit log |
| SSRF to internal services or cloud metadata | Private ranges blocked by default; metadata endpoints always blocked; DNS through host resolver; redirects re-checked |
| Host crash or resource starvation | Out of process; cgroups; per-call timeouts; concurrency limits; quarantine |
| XSS through visualization output | Spec-only output, validated against schema; iframe runs without same-origin under strict CSP |
| Tampered package | Signed packages, digest pinned in registry and checked on every load |
| Privilege creep on upgrade | Capability diff requires re-approval |
| Confused deputy | User-data APIs exist only on `CallContext`, with scoped grants |
| Secrets leaking into logs | Secrets never in env or config; resolved values redacted from plugin logs |
| Another process impersonating the plugin | Inherited file descriptor plus one-time instance token |

