## 5. Sandboxed execution boundary

### 5.1 Threat model

| Threat | Example | Primary controls |
|---|---|---|
| Buggy plugin | Infinite loop, memory leak, crash | Separate process, cgroup CPU/memory limits, call deadlines, supervisor restart/quarantine |
| Over-reaching plugin | Reads datasets or reports it shouldn't | Capability Broker: grants ∩ caller permissions, per call |
| Data exfiltration | Sends report data to an arbitrary host | No network in the sandbox; brokered `net.fetch` with host allowlist; audit log |
| Cross-tenant leakage | Cached rows from tenant A served to tenant B | One worker per (plugin version × tenant); KV namespaced by tenant |
| Host compromise | Escapes to the host FS/process | Unprivileged uid, namespaces, seccomp, read-only rootfs, no host sockets except the RPC channel |
| Supply chain | Tampered package or dependency | Signed packages, digest verified at every load, vendored deps, SBOM scan at publish |
| Secret leakage | Plugin logs a token | Secrets fetched at runtime (never in env/config); host redacts known secret values from log streams |

### 5.2 Isolation model

- **Process per (plugin version × tenant).** In single-tenant deployments this collapses to one per plugin version. The unit of isolation is the OS process, never a language VM (`vm`, `isolated-vm`, and Python sub-interpreters are not security boundaries).
- **Concurrency inside a worker:** up to `maxConcurrency` concurrent invocations. The Router queues beyond that and rejects with `OVERLOADED` when the queue exceeds 100 or a deadline would be missed.

### 5.3 Enforcement (Linux production)

Launched via a thin launcher (nsjail recommended) with:

| Resource | Enforcement |
|---|---|
| Identity | Unique unprivileged uid/gid per worker, no capabilities, `no_new_privs` |
| Namespaces | pid, mount, ipc, uts, user, **net (no interfaces)** |
| Filesystem | Read-only rootfs of the language runtime image; package mounted read-only at `/plugin`; `/tmp` tmpfs sized to `tmpDiskMb`; nothing else |
| Syscalls | seccomp allowlist per runtime (deny `ptrace`, `mount`, `bpf`, raw sockets, etc.) |
| Memory / CPU / PIDs | cgroups v2: `memory.max = memoryMb`, `cpu.max` from `cpuMillicores`, `pids.max = 256` |
| Time | Per-call deadline enforced by Router; activation deadline by Lifecycle Manager |
| IPC | Only inherited fds: control channel (stdin/stdout, JSON-RPC, length-prefixed) + data channel (socketpair, Arrow IPC) + stderr (captured as logs) |

**Dev mode (Windows/macOS):** Job Objects or rlimits for memory/CPU and a separate process only. The CLI prints `SANDBOX: DEV (not a security boundary)`, and the host refuses to start in dev mode when `environment=production`.

### 5.4 Capability Broker (the only way out)

Every plugin → host call carries the `invocationToken` the host issued with the triggering call. The broker authorizes each call as:

```
allowed = manifestPermissionGranted(plugin, capability, scope)
       ∧ principalPermitted(invocation.principal, capability, scope)   // prevents confused deputy
       ∧ quotaAvailable(plugin, tenant, capability)
```

Background invocations (schedules, events) run as the installation's **service principal**, whose scope an admin sets at install and which can't exceed the plugin grants.

| Host API (plugin → host) | Permission | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `data.query(datasetRef, query)` → Arrow stream | `data:read:<glob>` | Row-level security of the principal applies |
| `data.write(datasetRef, arrowStream)` | `data:write:<glob>` | For materialized outputs only |
| `reports.get/list` · `reports.update` | `reports:read` · `reports:write` | |
| `net.fetch(request)` | `net:connect:<host[:port]>` | HTTP(S) only in v1. DNS resolved by the broker; private/link-local/metadata IPs blocked unless the host is explicitly granted; TLS verified; response size cap 50 MB; all calls audited |
| `storage.kv.get/set/delete/list` | `storage:kv` | Namespace `(tenant, pluginId)`, quota 50 MB, value ≤ 1 MB |
| `storage.blob.put/get/delete` | `storage:blob` | Quota 5 GB per tenant/plugin |
| `secrets.get(name)` | implicit for names in `secrets` | Values never logged; cached ≤ 5 min in the worker by the SDK |
| `events.publish/subscribe` | `events:*:<glob>` | Plugins may publish only under `plugin.<pluginId>.*` |
| `services.call(plugin, service, method, params)` | `services:call:<plugin>/<service>` | Host-mediated. Callee sees the original principal |
| `log.write`, `metrics.emit`, `trace` | none | Rate-limited (1,000 log lines/s); redacted |

### 5.5 Data plane

Tabular payloads (query results, transform inputs/outputs, export sources) go over **Apache Arrow IPC streams** on the data channel, never as JSON.
- Record batches ≤ 16 MB and ≤ 64k rows.
- **Credit-based flow control:** the receiver grants N batches at a time, so a slow consumer never makes the producer buffer unboundedly.
- Each stream is bound to a request ID and cancelled with it.
- Hard cap per invocation (host-configurable, default 2 GB streamed) → `RESULT_TOO_LARGE`.

### 5.6 Visualizations

v1 plugins return **Vega-Lite specs or SVG**, generated server-side. The host sanitizes SVG (strips scripts, event handlers, external refs) and renders specs with its own renderer, so no plugin JavaScript runs in users' browsers.
v1.1 (deferred): `ui:iframe` contributions in a `sandbox="allow-scripts"` iframe with no `allow-same-origin`, a strict CSP, and a postMessage bridge that exposes the same versioned API.

